  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1. A cold airmass builds into the region this \nweekend. Freeze Warnings are in effect through this morning. \nAnother round of frost is possible Sunday night into Monday \nmorning.\n\nWith clear skies and light northerly winds, a cold airmass is in\nplace with temperatures in the 30s and 40s, and temperatures\ndropping below freezing in portions of the Lehigh Valley, far\nnorthwest New Jersey, and the southern Poconos. Freeze Warnings\nremain in effect for these areas through 9 am.\n\nSunny and chilly today with highs about 10 degrees below normal,\ntopping off in the upper 50s. \n\nAnother cold night on tap for tonight, and additional frost\nand/or freeze headlines may be needed, especially for areas\nwhere a freeze does not occur this morning.\n\nTemperatures will then begin to slowly moderate into early \nnext week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Uncertainty persists regarding a Nor'easter \ndeveloping off the coast of the southern Mid-Atlantic that may \ncause unsettled conditions and hazardous marine impacts early \nnext week.\n\nThe base of a deep trough axis will pass through the region late\ntonight into Sunday. As it does so, the trough axis will then \ncutoff into an upper level low which will meander over the \nsouthern Mid-Atlantic coast into early next week. At the same \ntime, strong and expansive high pressure will be building over \nthe Ohio Valley. While confidence is increasing regarding a \nnor'easter developing off the North Carolina coast by Sunday \ninto Sunday night, it must be said there remains a vast amount \nof uncertainty with regards to potential impacts to our region \ninto next week. \n\nConsiderable differences amongst global/ensemble guidance \nexists with regards to just how close/long this system will \nget/last in our region. Relative to the previous forecast \ndiscussion, there has not been much change. The EC ensemble \nmember spread is fairly tightly clustered through the early part\nof next week, and then begins to diverge, but generally has the\nlow tracking northward and then meandering off the Mid-Atlantic\ncoast into mid or even late week. The GEFS members are similar \nuntil about mid-week when they begin to diverge more \nsubstantially. All of this being said, it doesn't appear likely \nthat there will be significant impacts inland from this system, \neven though they can't be entirely ruled out at this time. What \nis increasingly likely though is at least some impacts to \ncoastal communities with periods of gusty northeast winds, some \nshowers, and potentially some coastal flooding concerns, \npotentially for multiple days next week. We will continue to \nmonitor the progress of this developing system.",
